  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date, National Aeronautics and Space Administration shows $10.1B in contract obligations across 6,118 awards, with an average award size of $1.7M. These totals are partial because current-year totals are fiscal year to date. Obligations are down 36.0% year over year. Looking back across recent years, obligations ranged from $15B in FY21 and FY24 to $16.1B in FY23, with FY25 at $15.8B and FY22 at $15.2B. The FY2026 to date total of $10.1B is lower than each of those prior full-year figures. Spending remains concentrated in R&D and space-related manufacturing, with a handful of large vendors receiving the biggest shares of obligations this fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How big is NASA's contracting program this fiscal year?",
        "body": "NASA has $10.1B in contract obligations in FY2026 to date, spread over 6,118 awards and averaging $1.7M per award. These figures are fiscal year to date and therefore partial. Year over year obligations are down 36.0% compared with the prior period. The FY2026 to date total is below each recent full fiscal year figure, including $15B in FY21 and FY24, $15.2B in FY22, $16.1B in FY23, and $15.8B in FY25."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are the main types of contracts and who captures the largest shares?",
        "body": "The largest category by obligation is Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $4B, followed by Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $1.5B and Engineering Services at $657M. Other notable categories include Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) at $605M and Industrial Building Construction at $383M. On the vendor side, CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY leads with $1.7B, followed by THE BOEING COMPANY at $822M and THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC at $465M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
